# Mutation cases for "the scope decides WHETHER text lowers, never WHAT it
# lowers to".
#
# Run with `scripts/mutate sce-build/tests/mutations/\
# a_lowering_is_decided_by_the_scope_not_written_by_it.cases`.
#
# `LuaEngine`'s two per-session caches are keyed on the AUTHOR'S text and hold
# only lowerings that SUCCEEDED — a refusal returns before either map is
# written. They therefore serve a cached entry to a session that has declared
# more names since, and that is correct exactly while a successful lowering is
# unchanged by the scope growing. Until 2026-08-29 it was not: the frontend
# refused what its scope did not name and `EcmaScriptToLuaTransformer` answered
# it wrongly, so a first evaluation could succeed with the WRONG answer and a
# later declaration had to be able to displace it. That is what the caches'
# `scopeGeneration` field was for. With the rewriter retired a refusal is the
# engine's answer and nothing wrong reaches a cache, so the field guarded
# nothing a test could observe — three cases in
# `a_session_scope_is_what_the_frontend_answers_with.cases` SURVIVED twice for
# that reason, on 2026-08-29 and again on 2026-08-31.
#
# So the guard was removed and this premise put in its place, where it can be
# measured: `scope_obligation.rs`'s
# `a_lowering_that_succeeded_is_unchanged_by_a_larger_scope` lowers every
# expression in every tracked document against a ladder of growing scopes and
# requires the first success to be repeated verbatim by every larger one. It
# holds for a narrow reason — `DocumentScope` reaches lowering at exactly one
# place, `ecmascript::resolve`'s `read`, which asks `declares` and either
# continues or refuses — and the day that stops being true the caches need
# their guard back.
#
# ⚠ A premise is worth only as much as its instrument, and this instrument's
# failure mode is a VACUOUS pass: a ladder whose rungs are never compared
# reports a clean green having measured nothing. So both directions are
# attacked below. Two cases break the premise in the tree and require the test
# to say so; two break the INSTRUMENT and require its own floors to say so.
#
# Anchors are literal slices of the source; an edit that moves one is reported
# INCONCLUSIVE with the anchor quoted, never as a pass.

mutation_tests -p sce-build --test scope_obligation
mutation_targets sce-build/src/ecmascript/scope.rs
mutation_targets sce-build/tests/scope_obligation.rs
mutation_oracles sce-build/tests/scope_obligation.rs

# ── The premise itself, broken in the tree ──────────────────────────
#
# A scope that DROPS a name when it is offered one it already holds. This is
# the shape an author's `<data id="Math">` has, and it is the half of the
# ladder no stage can express: the stages only ever offer new names, so a
# defect that needed a repeat offer would walk past all five of them and be
# caught by the two rungs that re-declare.

mutation_case "re-declaring a name the scope already holds removes it" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/src/ecmascript/scope.rs",
     """    pub fn declare(&mut self, name: &str) {
        if !name.is_empty() {
            self.declared.insert(name.to_string());
        }
    }""",
     """    pub fn declare(&mut self, name: &str) {
        if !name.is_empty() && !self.declared.insert(name.to_string()) {
            self.declared.remove(name);
        }
    }""")
PY

# A scope that forgets everything it held whenever it grows. The loud
# direction, and it is here because the quiet one above shares its anchor: a
# repair that made `declare` monotonic for repeats while losing earlier names
# would satisfy the case above and be caught by this one.

mutation_case "a scope keeps only the name it was last offered" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/src/ecmascript/scope.rs",
     """    pub fn declare(&mut self, name: &str) {
        if !name.is_empty() {
            self.declared.insert(name.to_string());
        }
    }""",
     """    pub fn declare(&mut self, name: &str) {
        if !name.is_empty() {
            self.declared.clear();
            self.declared.insert(name.to_string());
        }
    }""")
PY

# ── The instrument, broken in the test ──────────────────────────────
#
# The ladder walked from the LARGEST scope down. Every rung still gets
# compared, so a count-only check would not move; what changes is that the
# pinned answer now comes from a scope no later rung contains, and the
# refusals a small scope legitimately produces become disagreements. A gate
# that reported green here would be one whose ladder order carried no meaning.

mutation_case "the ladder is walked from the largest scope down" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/tests/scope_obligation.rs",
     "            for rung in &ladder {",
     "            for rung in ladder.iter().rev() {")
PY

# The two rungs an AUTHOR'S growth produces, no longer counted as such. The
# comparisons still happen and the premise still holds, so the only thing that
# can notice is the floor that requires those rungs to be on the ladder at all
# — which is the floor's whole reason for existing.

mutation_case "no rung is marked as re-declaring" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/tests/scope_obligation.rs",
     """    out.push(Rung {
        label: "everything+redeclared",
        scope: shadowed,
        redeclares: true,
    });""",
     """    out.push(Rung {
        label: "everything+redeclared",
        scope: shadowed,
        redeclares: false,
    });""")
edit("sce-build/tests/scope_obligation.rs",
     """    out.push(Rung {
        label: "everything+redeclared+probe",
        scope: probed,
        redeclares: true,
    });""",
     """    out.push(Rung {
        label: "everything+redeclared+probe",
        scope: probed,
        redeclares: false,
    });""")
PY
